Subject: RE: [chrono] Angle between two Coordsys items
From: Radu Serban
Date: Fri, 24 Jan 2020 08:33:36 +0000

There are several ways to do this that I can think of.  All of these implicitly assume your assumptions hold (the rotation between the two frames is about their common Z axis).


  1.  Let q1 and q2 be the two quaternions.  Calculate the relative rotation between the two coordinate systems, then convert to a rotation vector and use the z component:
    double angle = ((!q1) * q2).Q_to_Rotv().z();
I think this will give you an angle in [-2*pi, 2*pi] so you may want to clamp it to [-pi,pi].

  2.  Similar, but converting to an angle and an axis of rotation:
    double alpha;
    ChVector<> axis;
    ((!q1) * q2).Q_to_AngAxis(alpha, axis);
    double angle = alpha * axis.z();
The multiplication by the z component of the axis of rotation is needed to get the correct sign.  Note that Q_to_AngAxis clamps the angle in [-pi,pi], unlike Q_to_Rotv.

  3.  If you already have the unit vectors {u,v,w} for the two frames, you can calculate the signed angle between u1 and u2, assuming w1==w2, with:
    double angle = std::atan2(Vdot(Vcross(u1, u2), w1), Vdot(u1, u2));
If you do not have them, you’ll need to construct the rotation matrices A1 and A2 from q1 and q2:
    ChMatrix33<> A1(q1);
    ChMatrix33<> A2(q2);
    auto u1 = A1.Get_A_Xaxis();
    auto u2 = A2.Get_A_Xaxis();
    auto w1 = A1.Get_A_Zaxis();

--Radu


