 simulations.
From: David Marten
Date: Tue, 13 Mar 2018 09:04:50 -0700 (PDT)

Hi all,

I am currently working on a fluid-structure coupled simulation using Chrono 
FEA.
The structures I am simulating are pretty stiff - and I always observed a 
degree of instability in my simulations.
I was looking for the reason for this behavior for quite some time and 
might have found it.

in the ChElementBeamEuler::GetStateBlock function the nodal field values 
are being calculated. In this function the nodal rotations are obtained via 
ChQuaternions. In the last step the rotations are converted into direction 
and angle using the ChQuaternion:.Q_to_AngAxis function:

inline void ChQuaternion<Real>::Q_to_AngAxis(Real& a_angle, ChVector<Real>& a_axis) const {

    if (fabs(data[0]) < 0.99999999) {

        Real arg = acos(data[0]);

        Real invsine = 1 / sin(arg);

        a_angle = 2 * arg;

        a_axis.x() = invsine * data[1];

        a_axis.y() = invsine * data[2];

        a_axis.z() = invsine * data[3];

        a_axis.Normalize();

    } else {

        a_axis.x() = 1;

        a_axis.y() = 0;

        a_axis.z() = 0;

        a_angle = 0;

    }

}


The problem that I observed comes from the fact that for very small angles the first if() condition is false (fabs(data[0]) < 0.99999999), and an angle of 0 is returned.

In my simulations I often have quite small deformation angles due to the stiff structures I am simulating - and the discontinuity arising from this function affects my whole simulation. After I changed the condition by allowing the data[0] value to approach 1 more closely I have very stable results now and the all problems disappeared.

