Subject: Re: [chrono] ChQuaternion - How to get the 4 parameters?
From: Radu Serban
Date: Sat, 22 Oct 2016 07:29:18 +0200

Hi Luca,

The underlying representation of ChQuaternion is 4 Reals (Real being the 
template parameter) and not an array of 4 Reals.  As such, there is no 
operator[] defined for ChQuaternion.  The member data is public, so you 
can directly access it:

ChQuaternion<> q = my_body->GetRot();
double e0 = q.e0;
double e1 = q.e1;
double e2 = q.e2;
double e3 = q.e3;

You can get the last 3 components as a ChVector<> using the method 
ChQuaternion::GetVector(), but note that this is a method of 
ChQuaternion and not of ChVector!  Also, we do not provide a method 
ChQuaternion::GetReal() because, as I indicated above, you can simply 
access the member variable e0.  As such, your function QuatToAngle 
should look something like:

double QuatToAngle(const ChQuaternion<>& q) {
   ChVector<> v = q.GetVector();
   double r = q.e0;
   ...
}

Having said that, I am not sure what you actually want this function to 
return.  You may also want to look at the ChQuaternion methods Q_to_Rotv 
(which returns the corresponding 3D axis of rotation as a vector with 
length equal to the angle of rotation) and Q_to_AngAxis (which returns 
separately the angle of rotation and the axis of rotations, the latter 
as a unit vector).

You would use these functions like this:

ChQuaternion<> q = my_body->GetRot();

// Get the rotation axis (with length equal to angle of rotation)
ChVector<> rotv = q.Q_to_Rotv();

// Get the angle of rotation and the axis of rotation
double angle;
ChVector<> axis;

// Get the angle of rotation and the axis of rotation
double angle;
ChVector<> axis;
q.Q_to_AngAxis(angle, axis);

I hope the above will help you implement your own conversion to whatever 
set of Euler angles you are interested in (the method 
ChQuaternion::Q_to_NasaAngles provides a conversion to the common 3-2-1 
angle sequence).

--Radu


