Scott,

Another thing to keep in mind is the cost of the collision detection 
phase, in particular that of the broad-phase.  The important setting 
here is the number of bins in the 3 directions (specified through 
system.GetSettings()->collision.bins_per_axis).  Some experimentation is 
needed to identify the optimal division. Judging by the snapshot you 
provided, load balancing as the simulation progresses might also become 
an issue in your case (there is no support for load balancing in the 
Chrono::Parallel broad-phase collision detection phase).  In other 
words, the optimal binning division may be different at different times 
during your simulation.

I see that you currently use 100 x 100 x 100 bins.  You should 
experiment with different values (maybe even change it at different 
stages of your simulation).  To measure the effect of this setting, you 
should profile your application. Alternatively, you can use in your 
simulation loop a call to a function such as the one I copied below.

--Radu

void Monitor(chrono::ChSystem* system) {
     double TIME = system->GetChTime();
     double STEP = system->GetTimerStep();
     double BROD = system->GetTimerCollisionBroad();
     double NARR = system->GetTimerCollisionNarrow();
     double SOLVER = system->GetTimerSolver();
     double UPDT = system->GetTimerUpdate();
     int BODS = system->GetNbodies();
     int CNTC = system->GetNcontacts();
     double RESID = 0;
     int REQ_ITS = 0;

int CNTC = system->GetNcontacts();
     double RESID = 0;
     int REQ_ITS = 0;
     if (chrono::ChSystemParallel* parallel_sys = 
dynamic_cast<chrono::ChSystemParallel*>(system)) {
         RESID = 
std::static_pointer_cast<chrono::ChIterativeSolverParallel>(system->GetSolver())->GetResidual();
         REQ_ITS = 
std::static_pointer_cast<chrono::ChIterativeSolverParallel>(system->GetSolver())->GetTotalIterations();
     }

     printf("   %8.5f | %7.4f | %7.4f | %7.4f | %7.4f | %7.4f | %7d | 
%7d | %7d | %7.4f\n", TIME, STEP, BROD, NARR, SOLVER,
         UPDT, BODS, CNTC, REQ_ITS, RESID);
}

