Subject: Re: Co simulation chrono with other software
From: Alessandro Tasora
Date: Mon, 12 Sep 2016 12:31:27 -0700 (PDT)

Hi Arun,

it is not clear what you are doing on the C++ side... have you modified 
demo_socket.cpp ? 

If not, remember that demo_socket.cp assumes that the other cosimulation 
code receives* 8 bytes *(for the *double *type pushed in the stream_out << 
a_out;)
and it assumes that other cosimulation code sends back *3*8 bytes* , that 
we consider as *3 double* types.

If you want to exchange 2 integers of int type: 
- int are usually 4 bytes so demo_socket will automatically send 4 bytes
- if you get back an int from the VB side (check this in your Visual Basic 
conversion functions, though) you need to receive 1*4 = 4 bytes.

Something like

        int a_out = 0;
        int a_in = 0;

        while (true) {
            // Send to the client

            std::vector<char> mbuffer;                     // zero length
            ChStreamOutBinaryVector stream_out(&mbuffer);  // wrap the 
buffer, for easy formatting

            stream_out << a_out;

            GetLog() << local_host.getHostName() << " will send a buffer of 
n." << stream_out.GetVector()->size()
                     << " bytes. \n";

            // -----> SEND!!!
            client->SendBuffer(*stream_out.GetVector());

            // Receive from the client
            int nbytes = 4 * 1;
            std::vector<char> rbuffer;
            rbuffer.resize(nbytes);                      // reserve to 
number of expected bytes
            ChStreamInBinaryVector stream_in(&rbuffer);  // wrap the

rbuffer.resize(nbytes);                      // reserve to 
number of expected bytes
            ChStreamInBinaryVector stream_in(&rbuffer);  // wrap the 
buffer, for easy formatting

            // -----> RECEIVE!!!
            int numBytes = client->ReceiveBuffer(*stream_in.GetVector(), 
nbytes);

            GetLog() << "Received " << numBytes << " bytes\n";

            stream_in >> a_in;
            GetLog() << " a_in = " << a_in << "\n";
        }

Finally, very important: 

- in the VB side, check if you are converting integers to bytes using the 
little endian or big endian formatting 

(Btw. Chrono has also platform-independent code to manage this endian-ness 
issue automatically, but it is not used here for simplicity.)

Alessandro Tasora


