Zach,

Shig is correct.  Also, once you define sine_func and assign it to the 
link as its "spe" function as he described, if you later want to change 
any of its parameter, you won't need a dynamic pointer cast (nor a 
static pointer cast for that matter).  Instead, you'll be able to simply 
make calls such as:
    sine_func->Set_freq(0.2);

You would use dynamic_pointer_cast if, for various reasons, you might 
end up with different types of functions assigned to such a link (for 
example, a program in which you control what type of "spe" function you 
assign to that link based on some program argument). In that case, you 
need to see if you can actually convert the shared pointer to the base 
class ChFunction (as returned by ChLinkEngine::Get_spe_funct) to a 
shared pointer of a particular type, before attempting to invoke methods 
that are only defined for that particular derived class.

Here's an example of what I mean by that:

// ...

std::shared_ptr<ChFunction> func;
if (foo)
    func = make_shared<ChFunction_Const>();
else
    func = make_shared<ChFunction_Sine>();

// ...

my_link->Set_spe_func(func);

// ...

if (auto cfunc = 
std::dynamic_pointer_cast<ChFunction_Const>(my_link->Get_spe_func())) {
    cfunc->Set_yconst(0.1);
} else if (sfunc = 
std::dynamic_pointer_cast<ChFunction_Sine>(my_link->Get_spe_func())) {
    sfunc->Set_freq(0.1);
    sfunc->Set_phase(0);
    sfunc->Set_amp(1);
}


Radu

