Hi Michael,

&nbsp;


To my knowledge, there is no software that will directly create the INP file you mention. However, you can use a general-purpose meshing software, i.e. Salome, and provide an input file with the required information that will be read by
 Chrono. So, in short, it is possible to generate any quad mesh from a mesh generation software and, with few modifications, feed it into Chrono to have your mesh ready for a simulation. Some intervention on your side will be needed though.


&nbsp;


Another option, if you have an analytical form of your geometry, is to and parametrically fit the ANCF shell elements. For example, if you have a sphere you can parameterize it in terms of the number elements. In addition, Chrono::Vehicle
 uses a script (I think it's in ANCFTire.cpp) to create a mesh out of a 2D profile made of spline functions.&nbsp;


&nbsp;


Antonio Recuero&nbsp;


