Subject: Re: [chrono] How to get a obj file?
From: Radu Serban
Date: Sun, 14 Aug 2016 12:07:14 -0500

Jiang,

Your adviser is correct: you can approximate the desired shape using 
several primitive collision shapes (e.g., boxes).  The thing to remember 
here is that you can have more than one collision shape in the collision 
model associated with a body.  All you'd need to do is specify the 
position and orientation of these collision shapes with respect to the 
body reference frame. Something along these lines:

auto body = std::make_shared<ChBody>();
// ...  set other body properties
// ...
int num_boxes = 10;
double hx = ...    //
double hy = ...    //  calculate half-dimensions of each box (based on 
num_boxes)
double hz = ...    //
body->GetCollisionModel()->ClearModel();
for (int ib = 0; ib < num_boxes; ++ib) {
    double angle = (ib * CH_C_PI) / (num_boxes - 1);
    ChVector<> loc = ...   // location of current box, as function of angle
    ChQuaternion<> rot = ... // orientation of current box, as function 
of angle
    body->GetCollisionModel()->AddBox(hx, hy, hz, loc, rot);
}
body->GetCollisionModel()->BuildModel();

In the code above, 'loc' and 'rot' are the location and orientation of a 
given collision box with respect to the reference frame of the body.

Of course, you may want to also add corresponding visualization assets 
(of type ChBoxShape in this case).

Radu


